/* ============================================================================
gradians-conversion.epher -- degrees and radians into gradians (gons)

Gradians divide the right angle into 100 parts, so a full circle is 400
gon. Surveying instruments in Europe and artillery sights read gradians;
the conversion is g = d * 200/180 = d * 10/9 from degrees, and
g = r * 200/pi from radians. The same numbers backwards are
d = g * 9/10. This is plain arithmetic over the builtin constants and
conversions (guide 1.13); nothing here is redefined.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/trigonometry/angles/gradians-conversion.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\trigonometry\angles\gradians-conversion.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/trigonometry/angles/gradians-conversion.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

The one knob to edit is the `degs` list below. Printed values carry about
12 significant digits.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ---------------------------------------------------------------
const degs = {27, 90, 180, 360}   // sample angles, in degrees

// ---- helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------
def togon(d) = d * 10 / 9          // degrees to gradians: 200/180 = 10/9
def todeg(g) = g * 9 / 10          // gradians back to degrees
def fromrad(r) = r * 200 / pi      // radians to gradians: 200 gon per pi rad

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Degree knobs to gradians: 90 deg is a right angle, which is 100 gon by
// definition, and a full turn of 360 deg is 400 gon.
for d in degs do togon(d)

// The landmark radians. fromrad(pi) exercises the builtin pi constant.
print("pi/2 rad =", fromrad(pi / 2), "gon")
print("pi rad =", fromrad(pi), "gon")
print("1 rad =", fromrad(1), "gon")

// And back: 100 gon is the right angle again, now read from the gradian
// side. 100 * 9/10 is 90 exactly.
print("100 gon =", todeg(100), "deg")

// Spot checks against the definitions.
print("90 deg = 100 gon:", togon(90) == 100)
print("pi rad = 200 gon:", abs(fromrad(pi) - 200) < 1e-12)
/* ---- expected output
= {27, 90, 180, 360}
= {30, 100, 200, 400}
= pi/2 rad = 100 gon
= pi rad = 200 gon
= 1 rad = 63.6619772368 gon
= 100 gon = 90 deg
= 90 deg = 100 gon: true
= pi rad = 200 gon: true
*/
